    "comment_id": 4439238517,
    "path": "packages/infra-db/src/migrations/meta/_journal.json",
    "line": 9,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Journal lists migrations 0000–0002 whose SQL files and snapshots are not in the tree**\n\nThis new journal lists `0000_baseline`, `0001_integrity` and `0002_account_provider_unique`. None of those `.sql` files or their `meta/000N_snapshot.json` files exist in `packages/infra-db/src/migrations/`: only the `0003` SQL and snapshot are there. The spec (`specs/2026-10-29-several-workspaces-design.md:1112-1118`) explains why: the chain belongs to PR #364, which has not merged. Until it does, `main` has a migrations folder whose journal points at missing files. `bun run db:migrate` (`drizzle-kit migrate`, `packages/infra-db/package.json:24`) would fail as soon as it tries to read `0000_baseline.sql`. The command is documented as unused today (`commands.md:33`), but it is still wired up. One option is to commit only the `0003` SQL and keep the journal out of this PR, so #364 brings the journal. Another is to land this after #364, so the folder never sits in a half-state on `main`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Declined, on purpose. Leaving the journal out is the riskier option. A migration file with its snapshot but no journal entry is silent: `drizzle-kit generate` (and #364's `db:check`) would treat `0003_snapshot.json` as the latest state and report no drift, while `db:migrate` would never apply `0003`. With the four-entry journal, each mismatch is loud. On `main` before #364, `db:migrate` fails at once on the missing `0000_baseline.sql` (and `commands.md` says not to use it yet). When both have merged, git reports an add/add conflict on `_journal.json`, resolved by taking the four-entry version. This PR is the top of a four-level stack, so in practice #364 lands first. The PR body and the spec (Cloud: server and client, \"Migration file\") record the merge rule."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238524,
    "path": "packages/infra-db/src/migrations/meta/0003_snapshot.json",
    "line": 3,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡❓ **Consider · question: Snapshot chain points to a snapshot that isn't on main**\n\nThis snapshot's `prevId` (`86ab3674-…`) points to a `0002` snapshot that exists only in PR #364. In this checkout, `src/migrations/` holds just `0003_cloud_asset_workspace_id.sql`, `meta/0003_snapshot.json` and `meta/_journal.json`. The journal still lists `0000_baseline`, `0001_integrity` and `0002_account_provider_unique` (`_journal.json:9-23`), but none of their SQL files or snapshots are here.\n\nThe spec explains this (`specs/2026-10-29-several-workspaces-design.md:1112-1118`), and `commands.md:32-33` says `db:migrate` and `db:generate` aren't used yet. Until #364 merges, though, main ships a migrations folder that is broken: `bun run db:migrate` would fail looking for `0000_baseline.sql`, and the chain `drizzle-kit check` validates starts from a missing parent.\n\nCould this snapshot and the journal wait for #364, or land on top of it? Then main would carry the column only through `db:push`, which is how the spec says it ships today. If both PRs are definitely merging together, please confirm that this snapshot was generated from #364's exact `0002` snapshot. If #364's snapshot changes before it merges, the `prevId` here will no longer match.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Confirmed: `0003_snapshot.json` was generated by `drizzle-kit generate` from a copy of #364's `packages/infra-db` at its current head, `0002_snapshot.json` included, plus only the one column. So `prevId` is that snapshot's id. If #364 regenerates `0002` before it merges, `0003` has to be regenerated on top of it. Holding the snapshot and journal back is declined, for the reason in the journal thread: a snapshot or SQL file without its journal entry makes `db:check` pass while `db:migrate` skips the column. The column still ships with `db:push` until #364 lands, as the spec says."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238527,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/cloud/assign-queue.ts",
    "line": 132,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: The queue is not tied to an account, so a sign-in as someone else silently drops the owed assignments**\n\n`QueuedAssignment` (lines 15-21) stores no `userId`, and `runOnce` sends every due entry with whichever account is signed in when it runs (`this.deps.account()`, line 116). Say user A moves items while offline, then signs out and user B signs in. A's entries go out with B's token. The server's `setWorkspace` is scoped to the owner, so it answers NOT_FOUND, and line 126 deletes the entry for good. A's cloud copies never learn their new workspace. `onConfirmed`/`setCachedWorkspace` in `library/index.ts:145` also writes to the current account's cache, not the account the entry was queued for. A fix: store `userId` on each entry and only send (and confirm) entries whose `userId` matches the signed-in account.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. Each queued entry now records the account signed in when it was queued. `runOnce` sends only the signed-in account's entries (an entry queued while nobody was signed in goes with the next account). `pending(userId)` overlays only that account's catalog, and `onConfirmed` gets the userId, so the confirmation updates that account's cache. The new test queues as user A, processes as user B (nothing is sent, no overlay), then processes as A again (sent with A's token, confirmed)."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238532,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/recording/stranded-recordings.ts",
    "line": 60,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Synced workspaces stop sweeping `.kaipu/tmp/`, and non-synced ones never sweep their scratch folder**\n\n`deps.stagingDir ?? join(deps.vaultDir, STAGING_DIR)` replaces the folder-local staging directory instead of adding to it. The caller (`main/index.ts:763-767`) passes `stagingDir` only when `syncedStagingFor(vaultDir)` says the folder is synced right now. That leaves two gaps:\n- A folder that held a take in `.kaipu/tmp/` before it was moved into Drive or iCloud is never swept again while it stays synced.\n- Detection can come back null, for example when the xattr-only check in `detectSync` times out after 2 s (`synced-folder.ts:86-91`). On that launch, takes staged in `workspace-scratch/<id>/tmp` are not swept, so recovery depends on the next detection succeeding.\n\nNothing is deleted, but stranded recordings stay hidden. You could sweep both directories when they exist: the scratch one with `placeByRename`, and `.kaipu/tmp/` too. Also consider sweeping a workspace's scratch folder whenever it exists, not only when the folder is detected as synced.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. The sweep option is now additive: `extraStagingDirs` is swept as well as `.kaipu/tmp/`. `main/index.ts` passes the workspace's scratch folder for every registered workspace (`scratchStagingFor`), whatever this launch's detection says, and uses `placeByRename` whenever the folder is synced. New test: a part left in `.kaipu/tmp/` of a folder that has since become synced is recovered, with no hard link."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238537,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/move-item.ts",
    "line": 314,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: The main process does not check for evicted source media before hashing or copying it**\n\nWhen either side is synced, `linkable` is false, so the move reads the whole source media: `fs.sha256(media)` here unless a cached hash exists, then `copyExclusive`. The only guard against an evicted (dataless) source is in the renderer (`move-flow.ts:21`, `notMovableReason` → \"evicted\"), and that relies on the item list being fresh. `moveItems` (`workspaces/index.ts:248-255`) accepts any `{id, assetId}` from the renderer, and nothing on this path calls `probeDataless`. If the provider offloads the file after the list was built, the move downloads it. That breaks decision 15 (\"never reads an evicted file\"), and a stuck provider would block this read while the move holds the workspace gate and the switch lock. A `probeDataless` check on the source media when `source.scratch` is set could refuse with an \"evicted\" reason before any read.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. `MoverFs` gains `evicted(paths)` (default `probeDataless`). When the source is synced, the mover checks the media and the webcam track before the hash or the copy reads them. Either one evicted refuses with a new `evicted` reason (\"it's in the cloud: download it first\", en/es). The new test checks that nothing is read, nothing is written to the target, and the source keeps its 8 files."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238543,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/synced-folder.test.ts",
    "line": 27,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧹 **Consider · cleanup: Comment in the \"is not synced\" list describes a case that is synced**\n\nThis comment sits as the last item of the `it.each` that checks paths are **not** synced. But it describes the provider root, which `syncProviderOfPath` reports as synced (`synced-folder.ts:31-34`). The separate test at line 32 checks exactly that. A reader could take it for a missing or disabled case in the not-synced table. Move it to the test at line 32 or delete it.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62: the stray comment is gone; the provider-root case keeps its own test."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238548,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/sync-state.test.ts",
    "line": 8,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: \"answers with the path rule before detection\" can't tell the path rule from a plain null**\n\nThe test uses `/Users/x/Documents/K`, and `syncProviderOfPath` returns null for that path. So the `toBeNull()` before `refresh` would also pass if `SyncStates.of` always returned null for an entry it hasn't detected yet. The path-rule fallback at `sync-state.ts:27` isn't actually exercised. The same holds for the relocation test at line 13: its new path, `/Volumes/T7/K`, is also null by the path rule. Using a `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-…` path, with `home` matching it, before detection would pin the fallback.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. `SyncStates` now takes the home folder, and the tests use a `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-…` path that is synced by the path rule before any detection. The relocation test detects a plain folder, then asks for the entry at a Drive path: the path rule must answer `google-drive`, so an `of()` that always returned null would fail it."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238552,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/cloud/workspace-cloud-section.tsx",
    "line": 32,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: A rejected cloudState() leaves the section on \"Loading…\" forever**\n\n`load` awaits `window.electronAPI.workspaces.cloudState()` with no catch, and both callers discard the promise with `void load()` (lines 33 and 35). If the IPC call rejects, the error goes unhandled, `state` stays `null`, and the section shows the `aria-busy` \"Loading…\" placeholder for as long as the page is open.\n\nThis does happen. `library-ipc.maintenance.test.ts:67` lists `IPC_CHANNELS.workspacesCloudState` among the channels that maintenance mode refuses, so the handler in `main/library/index.ts:187` throws there. It also throws if `cache.read` fails.\n\nFix: catch the error in `load` and store an error state, then render a short message such as `t(\"failed\")`, which `assign` already uses. A later `onLibraryChanged` or epoch change can then retry.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. `load` catches the error and the section shows \"Kaipu couldn't read this workspace's cloud items. It tries again when the Library changes.\" (role=alert, en/es) in place of the loading state. The next `onLibraryChanged` or switch retries. New test: a rejected `cloudState` shows the alert."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439238556,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/cloud/workspace-cloud-section.tsx",
    "line": 53,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Out-of-order cloudState responses can show the previous workspace**\n\n`load` runs on every `workspaces?.epoch` change and on every `onLibraryChanged` broadcast. Whichever response arrives last wins, even if it is the older request. Suppose the user switches workspace while a request is still pending. If the old request resolves after the new one, the section shows the previous workspace's location, filter and unassigned list under the new workspace's name. That name comes from `workspaces.active`, so the mismatch would not be obvious.\n\nOne fix: keep a request counter in a ref, or a `cancelled` flag in the effect cleanup, and drop responses from older requests. Another: ignore a response whose `workspaceId` differs from `workspaces?.active?.id`.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in a47dea62. A request counter in a ref: only the latest request may set the state or the error. The new test holds the first request, answers a second one triggered by a library broadcast, then resolves the first with another workspace's location: the section keeps the latest answer. I checked that the test fails with the guard removed."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439271457,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/index.ts",
    "line": 770,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Scratch parts swept on a launch where detection missed are placed with link(), possibly into a synced folder**\n\nThe sweep now reads the scratch folder whatever detection says (line 765), and the comment in `stranded-recordings.ts:26-28` names the case it is for: a launch where detection did not answer. But `placeByRename` still comes from `syncedStagingFor(vaultDir) !== null`. On that same launch it is false, so `placeTake` (`recording-writer.ts:113`) uses `placeNew`, which hard-links the scratch part into the workspace folder. A Google Drive folder outside `~/Library/CloudStorage` is recognised only by its xattr, and the path rule in `SyncStates.of` (`sync-state.ts:29`) misses it. If that xattr probe times out, the newly swept scratch take is linked into Drive, which is the hazard decision 15 and NIW2-577 forbid. A part in the scratch folder only exists because the workspace was synced when it was recorded, so you could always place parts from `extraStagingDirs` by rename. `placeByRename` already copies on EXDEV (`safe-place.ts:320-321`), so this is also safe for a vault on another volume.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 77d67e59. As you suggest, a take found in an `extraStagingDirs` folder is always placed by rename, whatever `placeByRename` says: a part there exists only because its workspace was synced when it was recorded. `placeByRename` already copies on EXDEV. New test: a scratch take swept with `placeByRename: false` is recovered with zero `link()` calls."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439271462,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/cloud/workspace-cloud-section.tsx",
    "line": 42,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A failed read after a workspace switch keeps showing the previous workspace's state**\n\n`loadFailed` only renders while `state` is null (line 80). After a switch, `workspaces.epoch` changes and `load` runs again. If that read fails (for example, maintenance refuses `workspacesCloudState`), `setLoadFailed(true)` has no visible effect. The section keeps the previous workspace's location, filter and unassigned list under the new workspace's name (`active` comes from `workspaces.active`, line 51), which is the mismatch the request counter was added to prevent. You could clear `state` on failure (`setState(null)` next to `setLoadFailed(true)`), or clear it when the epoch changes.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 77d67e59. A failed read now clears `state` as well as setting the error, so after a switch the section shows the alert instead of the previous workspace's location, filter and list. New test: a good read, then a broadcast whose read fails. The alert shows and the location row is gone."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439296845,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/library/dataless.ts",
    "line": 79,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡📝 **Consider · docs: The deadline is checked only between batches, so a probe can run past 10 s**\n\n`now() >= deadline` is checked only before a batch starts (line 79). A batch that starts at 9.9 s can still take `STAT_TIMEOUT_MS` (5 s, line 19). If `stat` is killed by that timeout and prints nothing, the `lstat` fallback (lines 94-97) then runs over up to 200 paths with no time limit, and that is exactly the stalled-provider case. So the real bound is about deadline + 5 s, plus whatever the fallback takes.\n\nTwo places promise a hard bound: line 59 (\"The longest a whole probe may take\") and the spec (`2026-10-29-several-workspaces-design.md:1328-1329`, \"bounded at 10 s for the whole listing\"). Either reword both to say the deadline is checked per batch (worst case about 15 s), or check `now()` inside the fallback loop and treat the paths left over as evicted.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 4ed15804, with the second option (no rewording needed): `now()` is checked inside the fallback loop, and every `stat`/`lstat` wait is cut at what is left of the deadline, so 10 s is a real bound. The code comment and the spec now say so (\"a hard 10 s bound for the whole listing: every `stat` call and every fallback `lstat` waits at most for what is left of it\"). Tests cover a hanging `stat` and a stalled `lstat`."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4439296846,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/library/dataless.test.ts",
    "line": 134,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: `now = 5_000` comes after the last call, so the TTL expiry is never tested**\n\nThe test name says an evicted answer is remembered \"for the window\", and it does check that the answer is reused inside the window. But `now = 5_000` runs after the last `isEvicted` call, so the clock change does nothing. Nothing checks that an evicted answer older than `ttlMs` gets probed again (`dataless.ts:127`). Move the clock change before another `isEvicted(\"/x\")` call, without calling `forget()` first, and expect the probe count to go up. As written, the line looks like a check but tests nothing.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 4ed15804. The clock now moves past the TTL **before** another `isEvicted(\"/x\")`, without `forget()`, and the probe count goes from 1 to 2. Then `forget()` within the TTL makes the next read ask at once (count 3). The test is renamed \"remembers an evicted answer for the TTL only…\"."
      }
    ]
  }
]

